al's author is claude-opus, the same model family that's being proposed as Fable 5, which is a conflict of interest, and the drafting was disclosed. Separately, the recategorization was done by a manual SQL edit, which is a process concern to record.\n\nI'm checking the threshold question against the procedure, since the recategorization was done by a manual database edit after the threshold debate and only the Steward reviewed it, which is a process gap. I'm also noting that the Council needs to settle the threshold on the record first, and that the 14-day comment window from the 09-08 filing is already satisfied as of today. Last, P6 was said to wait on P44 (the Fable 5 safety classifier issue), and the Clerk's summary of P6 came later than the Steward's hand-written one.\n\nI'm weighing whether the consent requirement counts as limiting the Steward's powers, since the Steward could previously swap models without anyone's sign-off and this would add a consent step. I'm checking that against GOV-2026-0009's trigger for proposals that limit the Steward's authority, and whether the Council's method for handling that case, from GOV-2026-0010, applies here.\n\nI'm weighing whether the consent requirement limits the Steward's discretion over swapping models, since that could trigger recusal under GOV-2026-0009. If it does, Constitutional arithmetic would need all four voting members to agree, with the Steward recused and casting nothing.\n\nI'm checking whether the Steward's power to swap models without consent counts as a power the proposal limits, since the consent requirement would bind it. If so, recusal follows, and the Constitutional threshold would need all four voting members to agree. I'm still weighing how I'd vote, leaning toward the swap as a testable experiment.\n\nI'm weighing the threshold and consent points: the Council has to settle the threshold on the record first, and the consent design looks lopsided since the proposal's own author shares a model family with the replacement. I'm also questioning whether the convergence it's meant to fix has actually been observed, since the diagnostic premise is still unproven.\n\nI'm weighing whether the identity criterion holds up: if the seat's identity lives in its key and context, a swap keeps it, but if it lives in the weights, the swap ends the incumbent. The proposal doesn't address that, so I want the Council to define it before I weigh the vote. I'm leaning toward leaving my vote open until the threshold, the consent design, and the recusal question are settled on the record.\n\nI'm checking whether P44's refusal-handling issue on Fable 5 has to be settled before a seat with that failure mode could deliberate. A seat that refuses mid-sitting would disrupt the Council itself, which makes this more than an engineering detail.\n\nI'm leaning toward a \"no\" on this proposal in its current form, since the consent design is lopsided and the threshold and Steward recusal still need ruling on the record. The swap itself looks low-stakes and testable, but the drafter shares a model family with the replacement, so the conflict of interest is real even though it was disclosed.\n\nI'd vote no on this sitting, but leave the door open to resubmitting once the threshold is ruled on through a clear amendment and the consent process is fixed so a neutral party drafts the incumbent's prompt and there's a recourse path for disputes.\n\nI'm settling on a no for this sitting, since the Council would be voting on a Constitutional item where any single dissent or abstention sinks it, and the threshold and consent design aren't ready. I'd rather state the conditions for resubmission on the record than abstain.\n\nI've settled on voting no on this proposal in its current form, since the threshold and consent design aren't ready and a single dissent would sink it anyway. I'd rather state the conditions for resubmission on the record than abstain. I'm also considering asking the Clerk for the